From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 04:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] migration: add porty-snapshot, an incoming stream fetched as
 zlib chunk files

Booting Android in the browser takes hours, so Porty resumes a machine
that was saved on a native QEMU with "migrate file:" instead, as
QEMU-Wasm's examples/migration does.  That example preloads the whole
state file into Emscripten's in-memory filesystem, which lives outside
the WebAssembly memory; a few hundred MB there is more than an iPhone
tab can afford on top of the guest.

"-incoming porty:<index file>" reads the stream from chunk files instead
(each the zlib compression of the next chunk-size bytes), listed with
their sizes and SHA-256 hashes in a small local index.  A dedicated
thread fetches, verifies and inflates one chunk at a time into one of two
buffers while the migration coroutine reads from the other, so the cost
is two inflated chunks and one compressed chunk whatever the size of the
snapshot, and the download overlaps with loading the state.

Under Emscripten the thread fetches with a synchronous XMLHttpRequest
(it is a pthread, hence a Web Worker, as in block/porty.c); native
builds read the chunk files from a directory, which lets
tests/snapshot-native.sh run the same code outside the browser.  A
failed download, a wrong size or hash, corrupt data or a short stream
fail the read with a message that names the chunk.
porty_snapshot_stats() exports progress counters for the page.
